WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.823
The vagus looks like one grey cable, running from the body up to the brainstem.

2
00:00:08.763 --> 00:00:10.323
Does it carry one message, or many?

3
00:00:11.383 --> 00:00:15.923
A sensory fibre that carries signals from the body toward the brain is called an

4
00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:16.923
afferent.

5
00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:20.797
The sense of the body's own internal state is interoception.

6
00:00:21.797 --> 00:00:24.417
Lung volume, blood pressure, how full the gut is.

7
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.550
Here is the result that starts the lesson.

8
00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:32.510
Light on one set of vagal afferents stops an anaesthetized mouse breathing.

9
00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:37.443
Light on another set, in the same nerve, makes it breathe fast and shallow.

10
00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:41.583
Opposite outputs, from two subsets of one nerve.

11
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:44.323
That is a reason to look for separate lines.

12
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:48.113
Here are three questions to ask of any population.

13
00:00:49.093 --> 00:00:50.933
First, what stimulus do the cells answer?

14
00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:55.327
Second, what turns that stimulus into electrical current?

15
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:59.347
Third, what changes when you drive or remove the cells?

16
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.830
Driving a population tests sufficiency.

17
00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:06.610
Removing it and seeing the output fail tests necessity.

18
00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:09.743
Each question needs its own evidence.

19
00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:11.843
Some stay open for years.

20
00:01:12.803 --> 00:01:15.623
An answer to one does not by itself answer another.

21
00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:21.963
How do researchers get hold of these cells?

22
00:01:22.783 --> 00:01:25.063
With a genetic label, usually a Cre line.

23
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:28.307
A label is a handle on a population.

24
00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:31.747
On its own it answers none of the three questions.

25
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:36.837
Its name may identify a receptor or channel those cells express.

26
00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:42.517
Labelling alone does not establish the natural stimulus, the protein's causal role

27
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:44.397
in transduction, or the output.

28
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:55.300
One worked case. Afferents labelled by GPR65 answer food arriving in the intestine.

29
00:01:56.383 --> 00:02:00.483
Driving the same cells with light suppressed stomach contractions in an

30
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:01.483
anaesthetized mouse.

31
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:04.337
This looks like a satiety line.

32
00:02:06.557 --> 00:02:09.717
In an awake mouse, what should driving them do to how much it eats?

33
00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:19.677
In the assays tested, driving them did not measurably change feeding.

34
00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:22.887
One population, three answers.

35
00:02:23.907 --> 00:02:26.827
Yes to the stimulus, yes to one output, no to another.

36
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.200
Every answer holds only for the organ, age, assay and anaesthetic state measured.

37
00:02:34.233 --> 00:02:35.953
Hold three questions in mind.

38
00:02:36.893 --> 00:02:38.093
Stimulus, channel, output.

39
00:02:38.913 --> 00:02:40.933
A handle alone answers none of them.
